diff --git a/docs/timers.md b/docs/timers.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..fb23b92
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/timers.md
@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# Per-question timers
+
+In quiz **Settings**, **Timer minutes** and **Timer seconds** are nonnegative
+whole-number fields, both defaulting to 0. Both zero disables the timer. Seconds
+may exceed 59; the duration is always `minutes * 60 + seconds`, and the display
+normalizes it to M:SS. Invalid, negative, fractional, or empty settings are
+rejected without changing the saved quiz.
+
+**Start a game** snapshots the timer along with the other game settings.
+Subsequent settings changes affect new games, not an in-progress talk. A timer
+is local configuration, not portable quiz content: `QuizTransfer` still exports
+exactly `title`, `number_of_winners`, and `questions`. Imports get the zero timer
+defaults; unknown timer keys in JSON are ignored.
+
+## Deadline and finish semantics
+
+- **Start** and **Next** save the current question's `opened_at` and the game's
+  absolute `question_closes_at` in one transaction. Untimed games have no
+  deadline. Waiting in the lobby, reconnecting, and reloading do not start or
+  reset a question's clock.
+- Expiry changes `open` to `closed`, increments the game revision once, and
+  broadcasts the same results as **Finish**. It neither advances to the next
+  question nor ends the quiz. Manual **Finish** still works early.
+- Deadline expiry is checked again inside the same game lock used for answers
+  and moderator transitions. An old worker instance cannot close a newer
+  question. Duplicate expiry checks and stale Finish requests are harmless.
+- An answer arriving at or after the deadline is rejected, even if the worker
+  has not swept yet. If the answer request finds an expired open question, it
+  commits the closure before raising the conflict; the last accepted pick is
+  preserved. Timing uses the server clock, never a player's clock.
+
+## Local worker and recovery
+
+`bin/serve` runs single-process Puma. Its `after_booted` hook starts `GameTimer`,
+a small local thread scanning indexed open deadlines every 250 ms. Each sweep
+runs in the Rails executor, releasing database connections afterward. It stops
+on Puma shutdown/restart. Rake tasks and asset compilation do not start it.
+There are no external services, additional processes, or new dependencies.
+
+SQLite is the durable schedule. No in-memory delayed job is authoritative.
+The worker closes due questions even if all browsers disconnect, and resumes
+expired deadlines after a server restart. Transient failures are logged and
+retried on the next sweep. State reads also reconcile expiration, so a client
+never needs to wait for a worker to reject late answers or recover state.
+
+Expiry is logically enforced at the deadline; the worker's result broadcast
+normally follows within 250 ms plus processing/network time. During server
+outage or client disconnection, results are recovered once service returns.
+
+## Moderator-only display
+
+Only the authenticated stage state contains `closes_at`. Public/player HTTP
+and Cable snapshots contain no timer/deadline fields, and the player view has
+no countdown element. The stage shows a high-contrast, tabular-digit M:SS
+countdown while a timed question is open, hiding it in all other phases.
+
+The browser estimates server time from `sampled_at` plus monotonic elapsed time
+(`performance.now()`), not its wall clock. Rendering updates the countdown
+alone rather than recreating question buttons every tick. At zero it reconciles
+state; it never sends a blind Finish that could close a newer question.
+The local server worker remains responsible for unattended expiry.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `test/models/timer_test.rb`: defaults/validation, no-timer behavior, persisted
+  openings, snapshots, exact expiry boundary, duplicate/stale expiry, early
+  Finish, fresh Next deadlines, late answers, unattended sweeps, export and
+  player-state exclusions.
+- `test/integration/timer_test.rb`: field labels/types/defaults, settings
+  persistence, validation errors, moderator authentication, player privacy,
+  expiry reconciliation, and stale Finish conflicts.
+- `test/system/timer_test.rb`: Chromium settings, M:SS, reload without reset,
+  manual Finish, automatic result broadcasts, disabled player options, no
+  player timer, and expiry with the moderator screen closed.
+
+Run `bin/verify`, `bin/rubocop`, and `bin/rails zeitwerk:check`.
+
+For production, start `bin/serve` with the documented environment, then use:
+
+```sh
+bundle exec ruby script/timer_smoke.rb create
+# Restart bin/serve with the same SECRET_KEY_BASE and storage directory.
+bundle exec ruby script/timer_smoke.rb check
+bundle exec ruby script/timer_smoke.rb cleanup
+```
+
+The production smoke creates only its own quiz. It checks real local Solid
+Cable delivery while player polling is blocked, no external browser requests,
+reload continuity, early Finish, and export exclusion. It leaves a timed
+question open for the restart check. `check` reads the local production SQLite
+database directly **before** visiting any game page, proving that the worker
+finished with no browsers connected (not just a GET triggering reconciliation).
+Screenshots and smoke state go under ignored `storage/verification/`. `APP_URL`
+can override the default `http://127.0.0.1:3000`.
+
+Verified locally: 61 model/request/channel tests (1,122 assertions), 15 Chromium
+system tests (287 assertions), RuboCop, eager loading, and offline dependency
+availability. Production checks passed restart both before and after a question
+deadline, unattended expiry, HTTP/arbitrary-host readiness, and local-only
+browser requests. All timer smoke quizzes were removed afterward.
